Signed runtime `/`: lower it to `@divTrunc`, or refuse it with a Koru diagnostic that names the operand's signedness?

Both beat what happens today, which is Zig's own error reaching the user verbatim —
"signed integers must use @divTrunc, @divFloor, or @divExact" — a raw host error with
Zig's builtin list in it.

The asymmetry that makes this a decision and not a bug report: koru's COMPTIME evaluator
already answers it one way. src/comptime_eval.zig uses `@divTrunc` for `.divide` and its
own comment calls that "the C-parity choice". The runtime emitter has no matching
lowering, so comptime and runtime disagree about the same operator. Characterised
2026-07-25: signed `/` fails, unsigned `/` works, signed `*` works.

The `%` half of this pin is SETTLED and landed 2026-09-17: `%` lowers to `@rem` in the
Zig spelling of the expression text (codegen_utils.rewriteZigExpr), which is decidable
without a type oracle because `@rem` is the same operator for an unsigned operand and
the only Zig accepts for a signed one. `/` is not decidable that way — `@divTrunc` is
truncating division and `/` is exact division, so which one is right depends on whether
the operand is an integer or a float, and the rewrite site carries text, not types. That
type oracle is the whole of what is owed here.

Options:
  - lower it, matching comptime_eval's C-parity choice — needs the operand type at the
    site (the emitter's parsed `.binary` nodes have no types today either);
  - refuse it in Koru, naming the signedness — this pin flips to MUST_ERROR with that
    diagnostic as its expectation.

Blocks: this test, and the class it stands for. The corpus has zero other tests dividing
